Good news: this bug is specific to glibc's tcache reusing same-size chunks LIFO — worth knowing, since it means the bug may not even reproduce under `make asan` (ASan's allocator uses quarantine/redzones, not immediate reuse). But `make test`/`make memcheck` both run the plain glibc-linked binary, so it'll hit reliably there.

Two real fixes, ranked by scope:

**Recommended — scope the "was freed" check to a time window (generation-scoped tracking)**

The root problem is that `was_freed`/`free_occurrences` answer "does this raw address appear *anywhere* in history" instead of "did *this specific allocation* get freed." The fix is to record a high-water mark in `freed_count` right before the vulnerable allocation, and only scan entries recorded *after* that mark — which by construction excludes any stale entry from an address that's since been reused, while still catching a genuine bug (a real free of `third_value` can only be recorded after it starts existing, so nothing is masked):

```c
/* Scoped variants of was_freed/free_occurrences: only consider frees
 * recorded at or after `since` (a freed_count high-water mark taken right
 * before the value under test was allocated). Needed because glibc's
 * tcache commonly hands a just-freed same-size chunk back to the very next
 * malloc -- an unscoped scan would false-positive on a *stale* entry for a
 * previous, unrelated allocation that happened to reuse the same address. */
static int was_freed_since(void *value, int since)
{
    for (int i = since; i < freed_count; i++)
        if (freed_values[i] == value)
            return 1;
    return 0;
}

static int free_occurrences_since(void *value, int since)
{
    int n = 0;
    for (int i = since; i < freed_count; i++)
        if (freed_values[i] == value)
            n++;
    return n;
}
```

And in `insert_overwrite_frees_old_value` (`tests/test_rbtree.c:494-506`), capture the mark right before the vulnerable allocation:

```c
        int since = freed_count;   /* old_value's slot may get reused below */
        char *third_value = malloc(8);
        assert(third_value);
        strcpy(third_value, "third");
        fault_malloc_arm(1);
        int rc = rb_insert(t, "dup", third_value);
        assert(fault_malloc_free_count() == 0);
        fault_malloc_disarm();
        assert(rc == 0);

        assert(rb_size(t) == 1);
        assert(free_occurrences_since(new_value, since) == 1);
        assert(!was_freed_since(third_value, since));
        assert(rb_find(t, "dup") == third_value);
```

This is the only vulnerable spot in the file — every other `was_freed`/`free_occurrences` call either checks immediately after its own free with nothing malloc'd in between, or runs after all frees for that case are already done, so neither has a window where a stale entry could alias a live pointer.

**Why not the alternatives:**
- Making the test buffers different sizes (8/16/24 bytes) doesn't actually fix anything — glibc's minimum chunk size means very small requests often round into the *same* tcache bin anyway, so it's a coincidence-based "fix" that could silently start failing again on a different glibc build. That's exactly the kind of thing that'd make the test *fragile*, not correct.
- Inserting a throwaway `malloc`+`free` "guard" to absorb the reused slot is similarly fragile and implementation-dependent.

**Heavier alternative worth flagging**: since `rb_delete` is coming in a later milestone and delete-heavy tests will interleave far more frees/mallocs than this one case does, you may eventually want `track_free` to tag each tracked value with a unique id (e.g. wrap values in a small `{int id; char data[N];}` struct, capture `id` before freeing) rather than tracking raw addresses at all — that's robust by construction instead of by scoping. It's a bigger refactor touching every case that uses `track_free`/`counting_free`, so I'd hold off unless you want to invest in it now.

